er-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368748} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0def3afa-d576-50f1-8f4d-9a100feddea9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20.865" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368755} {:answer/id #uuid "51562709-9f9c-5a8e-8f3f-8c0a9bcf1284" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.642" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368756} {:answer/id #uuid "f5408555-2964-519a-ac00-22c6ba706b8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27.015" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368757} {:answer/id #uuid "ba3edb59-b7f2-5bb1-8f1e-e2412393446e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15.384" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368758} {:answer/id #uuid "0009ec3a-fcee-5a3e-aec5-8427a93c937e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25.615" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368759}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0009ec3a-fcee-5a3e-aec5-8427a93c937e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25.615" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368759} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5882344-e7b3-544c-a382-4a513ba726c1" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368754} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c53c63b3-cb30-5b5a-a0ee-88e338bea137" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368761} {:answer/id #uuid "146ec38f-1db9-5e20-aa91-dc96b377f801" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368762} {:answer/id #uuid "6b34cb64-269d-5a7d-bed4-c0ab429d853a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368763} {:answer/id #uuid "6cda34dc-9065-5361-96e4-b46b4d947db3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368764} {:answer/id #uuid "b850dabc-001c-55a7-8827-cb315ea21ee8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "146ec38f-1db9-5e20-aa91-dc96b377f801" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368762} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab4f2bcc-2b9e-514c-8036-82961daeef1f" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368760}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b4af4f8d-8886-57f9-92e8-20fda99f6128" :question/math-academy-id "q-334709" :question/problem "The local truncation error (LTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=4(Δx)^{3}-(Δx)^{4}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.3,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.1,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.3$ to $Δx=0.1,$ the LTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the LTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.3,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.3) & =4(0.3)^{3}-(0.3)^{4} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0.027-0.008\\,1 \\\\  & =0.108-0.008\\,1 \\\\  & =0.099\\,9 \\\\  & ≈0.100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.1,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.1) & =4(0.1)^{3}-(0.1)^{4} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0.001-0.000\\,1 \\\\  & =0.004-0.000\\,1 \\\\  & =0.003\\,9 \\\\  & ≈0.004.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.3$ to $0.1.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.3}{0.1}=3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={3}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.099\\,9$ to $0.003\\,9.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.099\\,9}{0.003\\,9}=25.615\\,38…≈25.615.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{p+1} & ≈25.615\\,38… \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{3}⁡(25.615\\,38…) \\\\ p+1 & ≈2.952… \\\\ p & ≈1.952….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$2.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the LTE:\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=4(Δx)^{3}-(Δx)^{4}\\,⇒\\,τ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{3})=O\\,((Δx)^{2+1}),\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368766 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11217aba-7942-5c3a-84cf-37f22aa209e3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method Given the GTE or LTE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc59a1bd-160b-5268-b8a4-f39672790e8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368768} {:answer/id #uuid "0246573b-45be-5d2f-9916-7220cb827721" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368769} {:answer/id #uuid "01893c0e-4c99-52f0-ace6-82948ccff5a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368770} {:answer/id #uuid "951f6984-43b8-5da7-97fa-2a3cc56f1f6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368771} {:answer/id #uuid "70f1c3a4-5ce9-57e2-86c5-70d163f4e673" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0246573b-45be-5d2f-9916-7220cb827721" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368769} :answer-field/id #uuid "6efbb6c0-19cd-552f-925b-e5efb27d5e13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f1914cac-7ae3-570e-a4f2-22c752a44bc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-334745" :question/problem "Which of the following global truncation error expressions represent a fourth-order numerical method?\n\n- $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{7}$\n- $e(Δx)=11(Δx)^{5}+0.5(Δx)^{6}$\n- $e(Δx)=0.2Δx+6(Δx)^{4}$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{7},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $4(Δx)^{4}.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=11(Δx)^{5}+0.5(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $11(Δx)^{5}.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $5$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=0.2Δx+6(Δx)^{4},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.2Δx.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368773 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4c7f962-87af-53af-8167-d24ade127366" :knowledge-point/title "Making Inferences Given the Order of a Numerical Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "215cf110-fe03-5dfe-ba6f-845edaf47df6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.001" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368775} {:answer/id #uuid "f469eba0-8fec-5eea-aec0-98af6f11effc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.004" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "215cf110-fe03-5dfe-ba6f-845edaf47df6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.001" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368775} :answer-field/id #uuid "52ce3212-4b73-5564-bd7d-3b5911d2d3c0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f94f46ea-01d5-5b38-8fa9-af08419febda" :question/math-academy-id "q-334947" :question/problem "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $4.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.4$ produces a local truncation error (LTE) of $τ(0.4)≈1.024.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.1,$ what LTE should we expect?\n\n$τ(0.1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.4$ to $0.1.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.4}{0.1}=4.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $4,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{4+1}={4}^{5}=1024.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.1,$ the expected LTE is\n\n$$\nτ(0.1)≈\\frac{1.024}{1024}=0.001.\n$$"}]}
